AUX-CONTEXT|<work>/refute-out/run/aux_src_single_sided_lp_AbstractSingleSidedLP_sol_convertToAssets_196.txt
Tracing the actual flow:

  1. The escrow branch in convertToAssets is not an "inflation" primitive by itself — it is the intended valuation path
  for an account whose position has been exited into escrowed withdraw requests. The claim asserts the escrow number is
  "inflated" but never names the arithmetic or state divergence that makes it exceed the normal super.convertToAssets
  value. Nothing in getWithdrawRequestValue computes anything but the per-manager value of the account's own request for
  shares.
  2. The "dust request on one token" trick provably fails on the actual code path. hasPendingWithdrawals returns true if
  ANY token's manager reports a non-zero requestId, but getWithdrawRequestValue loops over ALL of TOKENS() (for the
  derived CurveConvex2Token, both TOKEN_1 and TOKEN_2), calls manager.getWithdrawRequestValue(...) for each, and
  executes require(hasRequest); unconditionally for every token. So the exact state the attacker is described as
  engineering — a partial/dust request so that hasPendingWithdrawals flips true — makes the escrow branch REVERT on the
  missing token's require(hasRequest), not return a favorable number. The two functions have deliberately asymmetric
  quantifiers (any vs. all), and that asymmetry fails closed.
  3. Moreover, a request cannot be created for one token in isolation through the vault's own path: __initiateWithdraw
  first calls _unstakeAndExitPool with isSingleSided: false (proportional exit), then initiateWithdraw iterates every
  exit balance and opens a request for each non-zero balance. A proportional exit of a 2-token Curve pool yields
  non-zero balances for both, so both requests exist or the flow is not a "dust" partial at all. Manager-level
  initiateWithdraw also "will revert if there is already a pending withdraw," blocking stacking.
  4. The re-entrancy premise itself is unestablished. _executeDepositTrades builds a Trade and routes it through
  _executeTrade(trade, t.dexId) — the trading module, whose comment in this very file notes it "requires explicit
  permission for every token that can be sold by an address," i.e. dexId/exchangeData are constrained to permissioned
  adapters, not an arbitrary attacker callback target. The claim needs an arbitrary-callee hop that neither the base
  file nor the derived CurveConvex2Token slice contains, and CannotEnterPosition is imported here precisely for the
  enter-while-pending guard in the deposit path.
  5. Finally, even granting a callback, the attacker would be mid-deposit with t_CurrentAccount set to themselves and a
  pending request — the combination that step 2 shows reverts. No step of the chain both survives and produces a larger
  collateral number than the honest valuation.

  Every candidate step either reverts on an explicit require, depends on permissioned trading-module routing, or leaves
  the claimed "inflation" entirely unquantified. Under the tie-break this cannot survive.

  CONSTRAINT|C21|A valuation-branch-switching claim must show that the alternate branch actually returns a larger number
  than the default one and that the state needed to trigger it does not itself trip an all-elements consistency check
  that the trigger condition only tests existentially.
  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/single-sided-lp/AbstractSingleSidedLP.sol:convertToAssets:196|C21|The escrow trigger
  hasPendingWithdrawals is existential (any token with a request) while the escrow valuation getWithdrawRequestValue is
  universal with require(hasRequest) for every token in TOKENS(), so the claimed dust/partial-request state makes the
  branch revert rather than return an inflated collateral value — and the claim never identifies any actual inflation
  mechanism.
